        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because severe medical incapacitation discharges the duty to perform personal services, but the owner may offset completion damages against restitution under Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 262 and 377. Under Restatement § 262, the death or severe, prolonged physical incapacitation of a person necessary for the performance of a personal duty discharges the promisor's duty under the doctrine of impossibility/impracticability; the nonperformance is not an actionable breach of contract. Under Restatement § 377, a party whose duty is discharged by impracticability after rendering partial performance is entitled to restitution for the value of the benefit conferred. However, the measure of restitution cannot exceed the ratable contract price and is subject to an equitable offset for the extra expense reasonably incurred by the non-breaching owner to obtain full performance from a replacement contractor ($28,000 completion cost minus the $20,000 unpaid contract balance = $8,000 net extra cost). The museum may offset this $8,000 against any restitutionary recovery. (A) is incorrect because physical medical incapacitation discharges the duty, preventing the carver from being labeled a breaching wrongdoer liable for pure contract expectation damages. (B) is incorrect because restitution is an equitable remedy that will not force an innocent owner to pay more than the original contract price to complete the work. (C) is incorrect because restitution is available to prevent unjust enrichment."
