        fp: "An environmental research foundation hired a licensed commercial aviation firm to conduct aerial spraying of an organic chemical defoliant over a 300-acre private timber plantation to suppress broadleaf weeds. The chemical was classified as an eye and skin irritant, but non-toxic to mammals under normal exposure. The pilot complied with all FAA low-altitude flight regulations, operated modern drift-reduction spray nozzles, and monitored local weather stations. During a low pass, an unprecedented localized microburst wind shear forced a portion of the atomized mist downward and across the property line. The chemical mist settled over an adjacent high-density outdoor commercial trout hatchery. The chemical contaminated the oxygenation raceways, suffocating 50,000 prize rainbow trout fingerlings and causing $90,000 in commercial losses. The trout hatchery owner sued the aviation firm under strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity.",
