        exp: "(B) is the best response, because a prospective purchaser test-driving a vehicle is an independent bailee rather than an employee or agent of the dealer. Vicarious liability under respondeat superior requires a master-servant (employer-employee) or principal-agent relationship, where the tortfeasor acts on behalf of the principal and subject to the principal's control. In a motor vehicle test-drive, the relationship between a car dealership and a prospective purchaser is that of bailor and bailee, not principal and agent. The customer is driving for his own personal purpose of evaluating the vehicle, not conducting business on behalf of the dealer. In the absence of an owner-consent statute or negligent entrustment (neither of which is present here), a commercial vehicle owner is not vicariously liable at common law for the ordinary negligence of a test-driving prospective buyer. (A) is incorrect because the mere physical presence of a salesperson in the passenger seat to provide directions and answer questions does not transform a test-drive into an agency relationship where the dealer exercises legal control over driving mechanics. (C) is incorrect because a test-drive is an arm's-length negotiation between potential seller and buyer, not a joint venture sharing a common business purpose and joint control. (D) is incorrect because vicarious liability applies broadly across all employment relationships, not merely freight carriers."
