        fp: "Defendant wanted to break into an occupied, single-family residential home at night to steal firearms. At 2:00 AM, Defendant approached the residence carrying an iron pry bar. Defendant reached a ground-floor screened porch that was permanently attached to the home and enclosed with heavy wooden framing and aluminum insect screening. The porch opened directly into the homeowner's kitchen through an interior locked wooden door. Defendant used a utility knife to slice a three-foot slit in the aluminum screening, reached his arm inside to unlatch the exterior screen door's hook, swung the door open, and stepped with both feet onto the porch floor holding his pry bar. As Defendant walked toward the interior kitchen door, the homeowner switched on the exterior floodlights and appeared at the kitchen window holding a rifle. Defendant panicked, dropped the pry bar on the porch floor, turned around, and fled across the yard without touching the kitchen door or any personal property. The jurisdiction adheres strictly to traditional common law criminal definitions.",
